From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hero Siege Offline Toolkit <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Freeze only this process's threads around a hook write

Why: Every hook install and removal suspends the rest of the process
while SafetyHook rewrites the target, and upstream finds those threads
by taking a Toolhelp snapshot of every thread on the system, once to
suspend and once more to resume. On a desktop that is two walks over
thousands of threads per hook, paid on the game's frame thread: Hero
Siege holds its main menu at start-up and hitches in play while ForgePact
installs its hooks. The freeze now lists this process's threads only,
through ntdll's NtGetNextThread resolved by name, so its cost no longer
grows with the system's thread count. It also closes three gaps in
upstream's walk: it repeats the walk until a pass meets no thread it has
not already suspended (a thread started during upstream's single pass
keeps running), it waits with GetThreadContext until each suspension has
taken effect (SuspendThread only queues it), and the resume resumes
exactly the threads this freeze suspended, once each, where upstream
resumes every thread of the process, so a thread created suspended in
the window was started early and a thread suspended by someone else got
an unmatched resume. MmpFreezeCurrentProcess and MmpResumeCurrentProcess
keep their names, signatures and every call site; their bodies call a
new unit, thread_freeze.cpp, that needs Windows SDK headers only, so the
host test runs the code the DLL runs. No exported function and no
plugin-facing header changes, and hooks attach exactly as before.

Evidence: ForgePact #151's Live 1 (2026-10-06, ForgePact
docs/setup-stall-research.md, "Live 1"), measured with ForgePact's own
`incident setup` timer: a start-up setup of 1268.7 ms, of which 1237.0 ms
(97.5%) was 18 MmCreateHook detours, 68.7 ms each, against a median of
32.8 ms for one system-wide thread snapshot timed in the same session -
2.10 times, the two snapshots plus about 3 ms of other work. In play,
`dropmult relic 2` installed 20 hooks in town and the frame monitor
judged one 1546.5 ms frame. Static reading of upstream v2.0.2 (Aurie's
own source, 2026-10-06): the freeze and the resume each call
ElForEachThread, whose CreateToolhelp32Snapshot(TH32CS_SNAPTHREAD, ...)
ignores its process id and lists every thread on the system; the freeze
suspends each thread of this process in that one list and ignores every
failure; the resume takes a new snapshot and resumes every thread of the
process but the caller; nothing waits for a suspension to land. The host
test Aurie/hs-tests/thread_freeze_test.cpp runs upstream's walk (moved
into the unit unchanged) and the new one against 4 spinning workers and
a child process holding 1100 idle threads. With the unit holding
upstream's walk only, the 4 baseline cases passed and all 6 target cases
failed (the five freeze cases and target-logs-once); with the
per-process walk all 10 cases pass, and on the build machine the
freeze+resume medians over 9 runs were 0.21 ms per-process
against 103 to 156 ms for the snapshot pair. NOT measured: the cost
inside the game, which is the hub README's launch gate.

Fails-safe: When NtGetNextThread cannot be resolved, no thread-local slot
or handle storage can be had, the process has more threads than one
freeze holds (1024), or NtGetNextThread returns an error before the end
of the thread list (a thread it cannot open would hide the ones after
it), that call gives back what it suspended and runs upstream's walk,
unchanged, and the first such call writes one line once its threads
are resumed, naming the reason. Everything that can allocate, log or
take the loader lock happens before the first SuspendThread or after
the last ResumeThread.
The behaviour upstream already has and this patch keeps is in the hub
README's Known limitations: two threads freezing at once can suspend
each other (ForgePact installs from its frame thread only), a thread
suspended while holding the heap or loader lock, a thread stopped inside
the bytes being rewritten, and threads another process starts after the
last pass.

Log-markers: "MmCreateHook freeze: per-process thread walk", "FELL BACK to the system-wide thread snapshot"

Upstream-status: not submitted. Offering it upstream is the owner's
decision; the patch is written against v2.0.2 so it could be.

